cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this package, please cite it with the metadata below."
title: "Context-Window Occupancy vs. Per-Token Separability — companion code, simulations and estimates"
type: software
version: "0.1.0"
repository-code: "https://github.com/ulissesflores/context-window-occupancy"
date-released: "2026-09-28"
authors:
  - family-names: "Flores"
    given-names: "Carlos Ulisses"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-6034-7765"
    affiliation: "Codex Hash Research Laboratory, Codex Hash Ltda., Brazil"
    website: "https://ulissesflores.com"
license:
  - Apache-2.0
  - CC-BY-4.0
abstract: >-
  Companion package of a technical case study of nuFormer, the transaction foundation model
  described by Braithwaite et al. (2025, arXiv:2507.23267), whose input is a fixed window of
  2,048 tokens. Under a fluid Gaussian model with an optimal reader (assumptions (i)-(v) of
  docs/THEORY.md), a saturated window carries K times the occupancy-weighted mean of the
  per-token separability rho_s = d_s^2 / k_s. Hence, with the window already saturated before the
  new source, adding a source whose per-token separability is below that mean lowers the ceiling
  of the AUC of any reader of the same window; in the mixed regime the threshold drops to
  theta times that mean, and without saturation even with the new source any informative source
  helps. The package ships the closed-form model with proofs, six pre-registered simulations (a
  displacement replication over 720 cells with 200,000 users per cell and 5 seeds; a literal
  token-budget test with two event costs, 14 and 55 tokens; and four follow-up families on the
  exponent of d, occupancy against rate weighting, event fusion and quotas), order-of-magnitude
  estimates of the training and serving infrastructure, a claims ledger, and a SHA-256
  provenance chain that is recomputed from disk. Illustrative orders of magnitude; not a replication of nuFormer.
keywords:
  - context window
  - window occupancy
  - per-token separability
  - transaction foundation model
  - nuFormer
  - tokenization
  - optimal reader
  - Neyman-Pearson
  - fractional knapsack
  - pre-registration
  - reproducibility
  - hash chain provenance
